  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how photoresponsive biomaterials can be controlled with localized light to change biological environments. A light beam aimed at the center activates different functions in surrounding material systems. One application is targeted drug delivery: purple carrier structures hold red bioactive molecules, and light exposure in a defined region appears to open or break the carriers, releasing the molecules only where illuminated. Another application is a dynamic, “4D” cell culture scaffold: a blue network surrounds pink cells, and light-sensitive components within the matrix can be altered so the environment changes over time.\n\nThe lower images show the effects after irradiation. In one case, released molecules and exposed ligands on the material surface appear to promote interactions with nearby cells. In the other, the blue scaffold becomes denser or reorganized after light-triggered degradation or remodeling of photodegradable linkers, changing the local architecture around the cells. Overall, the figure emphasizes that light can remotely and precisely control when and where biomaterials release signals, present ligands, or remodel their structure, enabling spatially targeted drug delivery and tunable cell culture environments.",
